9front TCP loopback splice: propagate peer close to writer

When two TCP conversations on the same kernel connect to each other on
loopback, /sys/src/9/ip/tcp.c tcpincoming() splices them via
tcpsplice(), which installs a bypass function (tcpbypass) on each side's
wq that copies blocks directly into the other side's rq.  This avoids
the cost of running the TCP state machine on packets that never leave
the kernel.

The splice handling does not propagate peer close to the writer.  When
one side calls "close" on its ctl file, tcpclose() (case Established
with tcb->bypass != nil) hangs up the OTHER side's rq and then calls
localclose() on itself, which clears its own bypass.  The other side's
wq is left intact, with the bypass kick still pointing at us.

When the other side's user then writes more data, qbwrite() in
sys/src/9/port/qio.c (line ~1027) calls the bypass without checking for
errors and returns blocklen(b) regardless of the outcome.  tcpbypass()
sees that o->ptcl->bypass is now nil (because we cleared it), takes the
"frees the block" branch and silently returns.  The writer sees a
successful write of len bytes that were actually dropped on the floor.
No error, no FIN, no RST -- writes keep "succeeding" forever.

This breaks Go test code that relies on the usual TCP close semantics
("after the peer closes its half, my next Write eventually fails").
Several net/http and crypto/tls tests use that pattern on loopback to
test cancellation and timeout paths.  See for example
net/http.TestServerNoWriteTimeout and
net/http.TestTransportReqCancelerCleanupOnRequestBodyWriteError, which
were filed as plan9-specific skips while this kernel bug exists.

The fix is small: when tcpsetstate(Closed) tears down a spliced
conversation, it should also hang up the OTHER side's wq so that the
other side's next user write returns an error from qbwrite() instead of
running the now-broken bypass.

--- a/sys/src/9/ip/tcp.c
+++ b/sys/src/9/ip/tcp.c
@@ -412,8 +412,15 @@ tcpsetstate(Conv *s, uchar newstate)
 		qclose(s->rq);
 		qclose(s->wq);
 		qclose(s->eq);
 		if(tcb->bypass != nil){
+			Conv *o = tcb->bypass;
+			Tcpctl *otcb = (Tcpctl*)o->ptcl;
+			if(otcb->bypass == s){
+				otcb->bypass = nil;
+				qsetbypass(o->wq, nil);
+				qhangup(o->wq, "connection closed");
+			}
 			tcb->bypass = nil;
 			qsetbypass(s->wq, nil);
 		}
 		break;

A defensive change in tcpbypass() that error()s when the splice peer is
gone is also safe and easier to reason about:

--- a/sys/src/9/ip/tcp.c
+++ b/sys/src/9/ip/tcp.c
@@ -1589,10 +1589,11 @@ static void
 tcpbypass(void *arg, Block *b)
 {
 	Conv *c = (Conv*)arg;
 	Tcpctl *tcb = (Tcpctl*)c->ptcl;
 	Conv *o = tcb->bypass;

 	if(o == nil || ((Tcpctl*)o->ptcl)->bypass != c){
 		freeblist(b);
-		return;
+		error("connection closed");
 	}
 	qbwrite(o->rq, b);
 }

With either fix, the loopback-close-probe at
misc/plan9/arm64/loopback-close-probe.go reports the writer Write()
failing within a few milliseconds of the peer's Close(), as on Linux
and the BSDs, instead of accepting 1 GiB of dropped data.

The same bug should exist in 9legacy, since the splice logic predates
the 9front fork.  This patch is offered as a starting point for both
kernels; the in-tree maintainers may prefer a slightly different
shape (e.g. clearing the bypass and hanging up the wq from within the
existing tcpclose() Established+bypass arm rather than from
tcpsetstate()).
